No `.csproj` found, so I'll give you the general recommendation.

**Lightest option: use `TimeProvider` (built into .NET 8+)**

`TimeProvider` is an abstract class already in the BCL — no new interface needed.

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

The optional parameter means **zero changes to existing callers** — `new InvoiceCalculator()` still works.

**In tests**, use the built-in fake:

```csharp
var fake = new Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

Add the NuGet package to your **test** project only:

```
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

**Why this is the lightest approach:**
- No custom `IClock` interface to define/maintain
- `TimeProvider` ships in the runtime (net8.0+)
- The `FakeTimeProvider` also supports advancing time (`Advance(TimeSpan)`)
- The optional constructor parameter avoids breaking existing consumers

If you're on **.NET 6/7**, define a minimal `IClock` interface instead — same pattern, just hand-rolled.

